            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for to take it out on someone, then the Mandarin for furious."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: tòngkuài",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tòngkuài — \"delighted; to one's heart's content\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒmen wán le yì tiān wán de hěn tòngkuài — We spent the whole day having fun and enjoyed every minute of it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tòngkuài is built from tòng 'to hurt' and 快 'glad', both inherited from Middle Chinese, thuwngH and khwaejH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'illness' and 'to hurt' and the second as what pleases the heart. The sense 'thoroughly' for the first is not given there."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying how it was."
            }
          ]
        },
